WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.961
This video is about running an agent after launch.

2
00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:13.000
Two words: observability, which is being able to see what the service is doing, and regression, which is finding out that a change made it worse before your users do.

3
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.234
Start with what you need to see.

4
00:00:16.284 --> 00:00:33.248
For every run: the full trace, with calls, arguments, results, tokens and timing; the outcome, which can be pass, fail, unknown or escalated to a person; and the version of everything that was involved, because the model, the prompt, the tools and the skills all change independently.

5
00:00:33.298 --> 00:00:44.469
For every day: volume, pass rate, cost per task and latency; gate approvals and denials; and the failure categories from your error analysis, counted automatically where a rule can detect them.

6
00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:50.261
In LangSmith all of this comes from the same tracing you have used since week one.

7
00:00:50.311 --> 00:00:53.881
The work is deciding which views the owner looks at.

8
00:00:54.631 --> 00:00:58.551
Agents regress for reasons that have nothing to do with your code.

9
00:00:58.601 --> 00:01:02.522
The provider updates the model behind the same name, and behavior shifts.

10
00:01:02.572 --> 00:01:06.161
Someone improves the prompt for one case and breaks three others.

11
00:01:06.211 --> 00:01:08.146
A tool changes its output format.

12
00:01:08.196 --> 00:01:10.793
The documents change, so retrieval returns different passages.

13
00:01:10.843 --> 00:01:15.095
Or users start asking for something new that your golden set never covered.

14
00:01:15.145 --> 00:01:21.050
Each of these has happened to every team that has run an agent for more than a month.

15
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.959
The defense is the regression run, and you already own the parts.

16
00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:38.935
Before any change goes live, a new prompt, a new model version, a new tool, re-indexed documents, run the golden set k times and compare the pass rate and the cost with the last known good result.

17
00:01:38.985 --> 00:01:42.442
Same or better: ship it, and record the new baseline.

18
00:01:42.492 --> 00:01:46.651
Worse: investigate, with error analysis, before anyone outside the team sees it.

19
00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:54.016
The whole run should be a single command, or a single button, or nobody will do it on a Friday afternoon.

20
00:01:54.766 --> 00:01:57.738
The golden set only covers what you thought of.

21
00:01:57.788 --> 00:01:59.416
For everything else, two habits.

22
00:01:59.466 --> 00:02:09.488
Sampling: a fixed share of real traces is read every week by the reviewer named in your memo, labeled, and added to the golden set when it reveals something new.

23
00:02:09.538 --> 00:02:19.896
Alerts: pass rate below a threshold, cost above a threshold, a tool called more than some number of times in a single run, or a gate denied twice in a row.

24
00:02:19.946 --> 00:02:23.925
Alerts are how an unattended agent earns its position on the dial.

25
00:02:23.975 --> 00:02:27.618
Without them the far right of the dial is not autonomy.

26
00:02:27.668 --> 00:02:28.625
It is neglect.

27
00:02:29.375 --> 00:02:36.188
In lab six you build the owner's dashboard with volume, pass rate, cost per task, latency and gate activity.

28
00:02:36.238 --> 00:02:43.412
You make one deliberate change to your agent, maybe a model swap, and run the regression, reporting before and after.

29
00:02:43.462 --> 00:02:47.385
And you define two alerts and show one of them firing.

30
00:02:47.435 --> 00:02:50.998
Next video: the decision an executive actually has to make.

31
00:02:51.048 --> 00:02:53.165
Build it, buy it, or wait.
